  "summary": "A distinctive building style along the East African coast blending African, Arab, Persian, and European influences.",
  "description": "Characterized by coral stone construction, intricate wooden carvings, ornate doorways, and internal courtyards. Developed through trade networks connecting East Africa with the Middle East, India, and Europe, creating a unique cosmopolitan architectural language.",
  "culturalContext": "Swahili coastal architecture — Lamu, Zanzibar's Stone Town, Kilwa's ruins — blends African, Arab, Persian and Indian craft into coral-stone mercantile towns: massive 18th-century houses with inward courtyards, carved Zanzibar doors (pinwheel brass spikes against elephants, fish-motif date-code), baraza benches, mangrove-pole ceilings and rooftop rain terraces. The Indian Ocean trade (Frankincense to porcelain) wrote itself into the walls; UNESCO-listed Stone Town and Lamu Old Town preserve the dhow-port urbanism.",
